WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.539 --> 00:00:02.540
Who exactly are the Birds of Prey?

00:00:02.540 --> 00:00:04.220
And what do they have to do with Harley Quinn?

00:00:04.320 --> 00:00:06.360
It's not as cut-and-dried as you might think.

00:00:06.580 --> 00:00:11.160
Join us as we explore DC's number one squad
of superheroines, from their origin story

00:00:11.179 --> 00:00:12.690
to their ever-changing lineup.

00:00:13.849 --> 00:00:17.701
From the Justice League to the Fantastic Four,
superpowered team-ups have been part of the

00:00:17.701 --> 00:00:18.900
genre for decades.

00:00:18.980 --> 00:00:23.340
Sometimes they headline in pairs, like Batman
and Superman in DC's long-running World's

00:00:23.340 --> 00:00:24.340
Finest title.

00:00:24.540 --> 00:00:29.820
Often, they're sprawling collections of aliens,
shapeshifters, super-soldiers, and psychics,

00:00:29.820 --> 00:00:30.740
as in the Avengers.

00:00:30.820 --> 00:00:35.660
Occasionally, they're a grab bag of misfits,
such as the Suicide Squad, the Outsiders,

00:00:35.740 --> 00:00:36.660
and the Doom Patrol.

00:00:36.740 --> 00:00:40.800
Generally speaking, most of these groups have
been around for a while, to the point of being

00:00:40.810 --> 00:00:43.780
adapted to other mediums — sometimes more
than once.

00:00:43.780 --> 00:00:46.850
The Birds of Prey, in contrast, are a fairly
new invention.

00:00:46.850 --> 00:00:52.121
Born in 1996, in the Chuck Dixon and Gary
Frank one-shot Black Canary/Oracle: Birds

00:00:52.121 --> 00:00:56.100
of Prey, the team didn't get their own ongoing
book until 1999.

00:00:56.380 --> 00:01:02.200
That run of comics, largely defined by writers
Dixon and Gail Simone, continued until 2009.

00:01:02.480 --> 00:01:06.880
Numerous reboots have flourished and floundered,
the most recent being Batgirl and the Birds

00:01:06.881 --> 00:01:12.920
of Prey, which debuted in 2016 as part of
DC's Rebirth event, but it was cancelled in

00:01:12.920 --> 00:01:13.640
May 2018.

00:01:13.800 --> 00:01:17.760
Despite this current absence of the team,
the Birds of Prey are still regarded as a

00:01:17.760 --> 00:01:22.820
major player on the DC stage, a designation
made ironclad by the Birds of Prey movie,

00:01:22.820 --> 00:01:25.340
however removed it might be from its comics
origins.

00:01:25.340 --> 00:01:28.840
Sure, the Birds are young compared to the
Justice League, but that doesn't mean they

00:01:28.840 --> 00:01:30.480
haven't made a splash of their own.

00:01:31.300 --> 00:01:34.800
As far as superhero names go, "Birds of Prey"
is pretty great.

00:01:34.810 --> 00:01:39.159
It implies the team's majority-to-all-female
lineup without being condescending.

00:01:39.159 --> 00:01:41.920
It stands out when compared to other DC team
names.

00:01:41.920 --> 00:01:46.280
It denotes the approach of the Birds themselves
to the criminals they pursue — that of hunters,

00:01:46.280 --> 00:01:49.900
confident in their ability to track, trap,
and capture their quarry.

00:01:50.060 --> 00:01:54.479
In a genre littered with twee, saccharine,
or off-puttingly seductive monikers for its

00:01:54.479 --> 00:01:58.560
heroines and their organizations, "Birds of
Prey" is a stand-out winner.

00:01:58.560 --> 00:02:02.400
Readers will not, however, find the name very
often within the comic's own pages.

00:02:02.400 --> 00:02:08.010
It wasn't until issue #86, published a full
six years after the series' inception, that

00:02:08.010 --> 00:02:11.099
anyone on the titular team actually spoke
the name aloud.

00:02:11.099 --> 00:02:15.190
Prior to unveiling the helicopter that she'd
bought for the team, Zinda Blake, aka Lady

00:02:15.190 --> 00:02:16.200
Blackhawk, maintained:

00:02:16.500 --> 00:02:17.200
"We need a name.

00:02:17.460 --> 00:02:19.340
This 'agents' thing it's got no zing.

00:02:19.540 --> 00:02:21.520
So, what about being the Birds of Prey?

00:02:21.760 --> 00:02:25.099
Canary and Blackhawk, that's easy, and the
name Huntress fits."

00:02:25.100 --> 00:02:29.580
Though the name did show up in its own title
and others, it's nowhere near as openly used

00:02:29.580 --> 00:02:32.400
as that of, say, the Avengers or the Teen
Titans.

00:02:32.560 --> 00:02:36.840
But given the Birds' more clandestine approach
to crime-fighting, that's likely for the best.

00:02:37.860 --> 00:02:39.580
So, who are the Birds of Prey?

00:02:39.800 --> 00:02:43.779
Their ranks have grown and shrunk dramatically
over the course of the group's existence.

00:02:43.779 --> 00:02:48.329
Its two constants have been the women who
headlined the 1996 one-shot, Black Canary

00:02:48.329 --> 00:02:51.880
and Oracle, at least until Barbara Gordon
became Batgirl again.

00:02:52.100 --> 00:02:56.510
Their relationship was as electric as it was
supportive, a complex clash between the former,

00:02:56.510 --> 00:03:01.340
a martial arts master with an idealist streak,
and the latter, a steely realist and master

00:03:01.340 --> 00:03:01.840
hacker.

00:03:01.980 --> 00:03:06.580
Tempered by everything from the travails of
superheroic romance to disability, their bond

00:03:06.580 --> 00:03:10.320
formed the foundation of what would go on
to become one of the DC universe's premiere

00:03:10.380 --> 00:03:10.880
teams.

00:03:11.060 --> 00:03:12.680
But who joined them in their crusade?

00:03:12.740 --> 00:03:17.660
Well, often, there was Huntress, a vigilante
with a crossbow and no patience for fools.

00:03:17.840 --> 00:03:22.400
And then there was Lady Blackhawk member of
a World War II-era group called the Blackhawks

00:03:22.400 --> 00:03:28.180
who was ripped from the 1950s after the Zero
Hour event and able to pilot just about anything.

00:03:28.260 --> 00:03:30.040
Many others floated in and out of the group.

00:03:30.380 --> 00:03:35.540
Big Barda, Katana, and Judomaster are but
a handful of those who've claimed Birds membership

00:03:35.540 --> 00:03:36.460
over the years.

00:03:36.520 --> 00:03:39.200
But beyond the main four, there were few who
stayed for long.

00:03:39.360 --> 00:03:43.440
This didn't just keep the team multitalented
and flexible, it made the book enduringly

00:03:43.440 --> 00:03:44.100
entertaining.

00:03:44.160 --> 00:03:47.960
Readers never knew who would show up next,
and what stories might arrive with them.

00:03:49.060 --> 00:03:53.510
Upon rummaging through the Birds' comics history,
curious movie fans will immediately realize

00:03:53.510 --> 00:03:54.260
something odd.

00:03:54.360 --> 00:03:59.180
Even though she's the lead of the 2019 movie,
Harley Quinn isn't really found within the

00:03:59.180 --> 00:04:01.530
Birds of Prey comics as a member of the team.

00:04:01.530 --> 00:04:05.590
Her friends and lovers put in an appearance
or two Poison Ivy was a member of the New

00:04:05.590 --> 00:04:10.629
52 Birds from the first issue, and Catwoman
headlined the Birds of Prey: Manhunt miniseries

00:04:10.629 --> 00:04:13.920
but Harley Quinn never does, at least not
in an official group capacity.

00:04:14.120 --> 00:04:18.200
Comics often go through changes on their journey
to cinematic adaptation, ranging from the

00:04:18.200 --> 00:04:19.360
minor to the massive.

00:04:19.560 --> 00:04:23.780
Remember when Halle Berry's Catwoman was brought
back from the dead by an Egyptian Mau, bearing

00:04:23.790 --> 00:04:25.760
abilities from the goddess Bast?

00:04:25.760 --> 00:04:30.770
Yet few anticipated a Birds of Prey movie
starring an antiheroine with little, if any,

00:04:30.770 --> 00:04:32.160
connection to the titular team.

00:04:32.300 --> 00:04:36.020
"Psychologically speaking, vengeance rarely
brings the catharsis we were hoping for."

00:04:36.220 --> 00:04:36.720
"Yeah."

00:04:37.080 --> 00:04:38.020
"Are we ready?"

00:04:38.080 --> 00:04:40.960
This isn't the only departure from the source
material, however.

00:04:41.100 --> 00:04:44.340
Cassandra Cain goes from Bat-family member
to street kid.

00:04:44.340 --> 00:04:49.160
Hard-bitten Gotham detective Renee Montoya
is in the mix, and criminal kingpin Black

00:04:49.160 --> 00:04:53.140
Mask, typically a Batman and Catwoman villain,
acts as the Birds' antagonist.

00:04:53.140 --> 00:04:55.600
But therein lies the beauty of the comic book
movie.

00:04:55.900 --> 00:04:57.980
It offers bright new visions of beloved favorites.

00:04:59.040 --> 00:05:03.280
Though the Birds of Prey are certainly best
known for being a heroic team full of do-gooders,

00:05:03.280 --> 00:05:06.720
their ranks have been filled out more than
once by those less morally inclined.

00:05:06.720 --> 00:05:11.920
Lady Shiva, a ruthless assassin and mother
of onetime Batgirl Cassandra Cain, was perhaps

00:05:11.920 --> 00:05:13.220
the best example of this.

00:05:13.300 --> 00:05:17.500
Brought to the Birds by the death of a sensei
that Shiva shared with Black Canary, she became

00:05:17.510 --> 00:05:19.160
an out-and-out protagonist.

00:05:19.160 --> 00:05:23.530
As a woman with a past involving violence
and an unceasing desire to push herself beyond

00:05:23.530 --> 00:05:27.370
any and all limits, Shiva fit well alongside
the series other characters.

00:05:27.370 --> 00:05:31.920
She never became squeaky clean, nor even all
that consistently heroic, but her heart could

00:05:31.920 --> 00:05:36.260
be glimpsed beneath the grime and blood more
often in the pages of Birds of Prey than ever

00:05:36.260 --> 00:05:36.760
before.

00:05:36.760 --> 00:05:39.560
Most affecting of all was her relationship
with Black Canary.

00:05:39.760 --> 00:05:43.880
To call them "friends" implies an ease and
closeness that doesn't quite fit, but their

00:05:43.880 --> 00:05:48.750
shared training and history brought them together
as drinking buddies, sparring partners, and

00:05:48.750 --> 00:05:50.180
sometimes-comrades.

00:05:50.180 --> 00:05:54.440
This willingness to embrace characters with
murky intentions as capable of greatness was

00:05:54.440 --> 00:05:58.810
the series' biggest strength, and truly what
made it a modern classic of DC Comics.

00:05:58.810 --> 00:06:03.060
What mattered in Birds of Prey wasn't where
Lady Shiva had been it was where she was going.

00:06:04.140 --> 00:06:08.960
For all its innovation, 2019's Birds of Prey
is not, in fact, the first live-action screen

00:06:08.960 --> 00:06:10.920
adaptation of the titular team.

00:06:11.120 --> 00:06:16.060
That honor belongs to the short-lived 2002
Birds of Prey TV series, which only aired

00:06:16.060 --> 00:06:18.700
13 episodes on the WB before its cancellation.

00:06:19.040 --> 00:06:21.820
And to say this adaptation was loose is an
understatement.

00:06:22.460 --> 00:06:23.180
"Dinah.

00:06:23.620 --> 00:06:30.020
A meta-human herself with powers to open hidden
doors to the mind, powers that she is only

00:06:30.020 --> 00:06:31.500
beginning to explore."

00:06:32.000 --> 00:06:36.160
Fascinatingly, this dramatic departure from
the comics canon had something in common with

00:06:36.160 --> 00:06:38.640
the Birds of the 2019 film: Harley Quinn.

00:06:38.980 --> 00:06:42.940
In the TV series, however, she wasn't the
gleeful misfit in the golden jumpsuit.

00:06:43.100 --> 00:06:45.400
She was being set up as the show's main antagonist

00:06:45.600 --> 00:06:49.430
Audiences never discovered how that story
would've turned out, but at least they ended

00:06:49.430 --> 00:06:51.850
up with a whole other Birds-linked Harley
to enjoy.

00:06:53.280 --> 00:06:57.240
Justice League Unlimited was beloved by fans
for its smart writing, bold storytelling,

00:06:57.240 --> 00:06:58.620
and crisp animation.

00:06:58.780 --> 00:07:02.760
But in a DC Animated Universe where those
sterling qualities are the norm, it takes

00:07:02.760 --> 00:07:05.130
a little something extra to put a show over
the top.

00:07:05.130 --> 00:07:09.280
For Justice League Unlimited, that came in
the form of its deep dive into the murkiest

00:07:09.280 --> 00:07:10.520
depths of DC lore.

00:07:10.680 --> 00:07:14.790
No character was too obscure to slip in, no
arc too weird to adapt.

00:07:14.790 --> 00:07:18.940
This was, of course, tremendously satisfying
for long-time fans, but also a delight to

00:07:18.940 --> 00:07:19.440
newbies.

00:07:19.440 --> 00:07:24.260
Many young viewers first encountered the likes
of Fire and Ice, Booster Gold, and Aztek in

00:07:24.260 --> 00:07:25.840
watching the celebrated series.

00:07:25.900 --> 00:07:30.300
Sadly, the Birds of Prey never managed to
make the cut but not, as it turns out, for

00:07:30.300 --> 00:07:31.020
lack of trying.

00:07:31.300 --> 00:07:35.500
Producer Dwayne McDuffie, having enjoyed the
comic series, pitched an episode centered

00:07:35.500 --> 00:07:36.300
around the team.

00:07:36.540 --> 00:07:40.660
Birds writer Gail Simone wrote a script in
which Barbara Gordon creates the persona of

00:07:40.660 --> 00:07:45.380
Oracle and works remotely with Huntress and
Black Canary, but the character rights involving

00:07:45.380 --> 00:07:48.740
Batgirl were so prohibitive that she had to
be taken out of the story.

00:07:49.020 --> 00:07:53.340
Not interested in making, quote, "a half-way
Birds of Prey," the crew decided to retool,

00:07:53.350 --> 00:07:58.350
and the episode "Double Date" became a beloved
caper involving Black Canary, Green Arrow,

00:07:58.350 --> 00:07:59.770
Huntress, and the Question.

00:07:59.770 --> 00:08:02.600
Too bad for the Birds, but very good for the
viewers.

00:08:02.680 --> 00:08:05.440
"And here I heard you some kind of brilliant
fighter."

00:08:10.160 --> 00:08:11.080
"You heard right."

00:08:11.940 --> 00:08:15.980
Batman: The Brave and the Bold was well-loved
for its whole-hearted devotion to the spirit

00:08:15.980 --> 00:08:16.800
of the Silver Age.

00:08:16.980 --> 00:08:21.740
After all, it was made by a cast and crew
eager to bring all their favorite DC characters

00:08:21.740 --> 00:08:22.560
into the mix.

00:08:22.760 --> 00:08:26.180
Naturally, the Birds of Prey showed up, but
not as one might expect.

00:08:26.400 --> 00:08:31.380
In an episode entitled "The Mask of Matches
Malone!," Black Canary, Huntress, and Catwoman

00:08:31.389 --> 00:08:35.729
found themselves cornered on stage at the
Iceberg Lunge, a waterhole for supervillains

00:08:35.729 --> 00:08:37.040
and their assorted henchman.

00:08:37.200 --> 00:08:41.479
Briefly frozen before the glitteringly evil
assemblage, the women rallied and burst into

00:08:41.480 --> 00:08:44.260
a close-harmony song titled "Birds of Prey."

00:08:44.920 --> 00:08:45.560
"Any ideas?"

00:08:45.860 --> 00:08:46.660
"Just one.

00:08:47.640 --> 00:08:48.140
Hit it."

00:08:48.360 --> 00:08:52.019
This song has more to do with the variously
superpowered men in their lives than anything

00:08:52.019 --> 00:08:56.300
they were doing themselves, and it was mostly
meant as a way to get through to a then-amnesiac

00:08:56.300 --> 00:08:56.820
Batman.

00:08:56.840 --> 00:09:02.200
But the song rocketed to online infamy immediately
after airing in Australia, due to the innuendo-laden

00:09:02.200 --> 00:09:02.700
lyrics.

00:09:02.740 --> 00:09:06.740
The buzz was so loud that the song had to
be edited before airing in the United States.

00:09:06.740 --> 00:09:11.579
But the internet's memory is long indeed,
and "Birds of Prey" and its sly commentary

00:09:11.580 --> 00:09:13.600
lives on in the hearts of fans worldwide.

00:09:14.740 --> 00:09:19.740
The DC universe's bad guys can be plenty scary,
what with the likes of the Joker, Lex Luthor,

00:09:19.740 --> 00:09:21.000
and Deadshot running around.

00:09:21.340 --> 00:09:25.020
Things can get pretty grim in a superheroic
universe, like the time the Joker cut off

00:09:25.020 --> 00:09:28.100
his own face and pinned it to his cell, just
to make a statement.

00:09:28.340 --> 00:09:32.240
The grotesque can gain a gargoyle's wings
in the realm of comic books, able to fly to

00:09:32.259 --> 00:09:33.550
new heights of horror.

00:09:33.550 --> 00:09:36.600
But the villainy of Birds of Prey was considerably
more downbeat.

00:09:36.680 --> 00:09:40.660
Oh sure, guys with names like the Calculator
squared off against the Birds more than once.

00:09:40.660 --> 00:09:44.360
But the evils the Birds faced were much more
in line with that of the real world.

00:09:44.360 --> 00:09:49.120
Human trafficking, terrorism, and human rights
abuses were the order of the day within those

00:09:49.120 --> 00:09:49.620
pages.

00:09:49.620 --> 00:09:54.320
The first story of the series, in fact, involved
the destabilization of a small country and

00:09:54.320 --> 00:09:57.920
its subsequent use as a lawless haven for
the disreputable elite.

00:09:57.920 --> 00:10:01.820
The Birds might have worn capes, but they
faced malice of a very familiar sort.

00:10:02.640 --> 00:10:04.640
Birds of Prey was massively ahead of the curve.

00:10:04.820 --> 00:10:09.740
Writer Gail Simone has gone on record as conceiving
of Black Canary as bisexual, though her attempts

00:10:09.740 --> 00:10:11.880
to make that textually overt were stymied.

00:10:12.160 --> 00:10:16.040
Nevertheless, she managed to make it clear
that her version of the Birds of Prey was

00:10:16.040 --> 00:10:21.430
one with LGBTQ characters, which was evidenced
most explicitly by the characters Savant and

00:10:21.430 --> 00:10:21.920
Creote.

00:10:21.940 --> 00:10:26.820
An entitled heir and a former KGB operative,
respectively, the villainous pair transformed

00:10:26.840 --> 00:10:29.060
into good guys over the course of the series.

00:10:29.060 --> 00:10:32.640
And when Creote's feelings for Savant were
revealed, they became a couple.

00:10:32.840 --> 00:10:34.060
Take note, Hollywood.

00:10:34.060 --> 00:10:36.000
Birds of Prey was way ahead of the curve.

00:10:36.280 --> 00:10:38.440
Check out one of our newest videos right here!

00:10:38.440 --> 00:10:41.840
Plus, even more Looper videos about your favorite
comics are coming soon.

00:10:42.060 --> 00:10:45.980
Subscribe to our YouTube channel and hit the
bell so you don't miss a single one.

